<!-- ============================ COVER ============================ -->
<section class="sheet cover">
  <img src="{{COVER_PAGE_URI}}" alt="Cover: the title The Meter Is Not the Money, subtitled how AI compute gets priced, metered, and paid for, by Anuj Sadani.">
</section>

<!-- ============================ TITLE PAGE ============================ -->
<section class="sheet titlepage">
  <p class="kicker">A verified inquiry &middot; 2026</p>
  <h1>The Meter<br>Is Not the<br>Money</h1>
  <div class="rule"></div>
  <p class="sub">How AI compute gets priced, metered, and paid for.</p>
  <p class="desc">In August 2026 a payments company agreed to buy a router for a
    price it never disclosed and the press could not agree on. Its chief executive
    justified the purchase in a single sentence about what a token now is.</p>
  <p class="desc">That sentence is half right, and the wrong half is the
    interesting one.
    Every credit balance in this market &mdash; including the one belonging to the
    company that was bought &mdash; is denominated in dollars, because a token's
    price is not fixed at the moment you buy it, varies by a factor of three
    between suppliers of an identical product, and moves in opposite directions
    depending on which vendor served the request.</p>
  <p class="desc">The conclusion is not that the deal was a mistake. It is that
    the token is a meter reading rather than a unit of account, that every credit
    is a conversion layer over dollars, and that the position worth owning is
    therefore the exchange desk rather than the mint.</p>
  <p class="byline">Anuj Sadani
    <small>Every claim bound to a captured source</small>
  </p>
  <p class="entry-links">
    <a class="entry-summary" href="summary/">Short on time? The argument in brief &mdash; 3&ndash;5 min read</a>
  </p>
  <a class="cover-dl" href="https://ko-fi.com/s/0f1f4f72ad" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="ar">&nearr;</span>&nbsp; Get the typeset PDF on Ko-fi</a>
</section>

<!-- BOOK-FORGE:FRONT-MATTER v1 sha=01ed27e6cdeb -->
<section class="bf-matter bf-front bf-flow">
  <h2>Copyright, permissions, and how this was made</h2>

  <div class="legal">
    <p class="work">The Meter Is Not the Money &mdash; a verified inquiry into whether the token can serve as a unit of account, and what a payments company bought when it bought a router.</p>

    <p>Copyright &copy; 2026 Anuj Sadani.</p>

    <div class="grant">
      <p>All rights reserved. Quote it in a review or a piece of criticism with
          attribution; anything further, please ask.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The sources are a separate matter. This book is published alongside a
      free companion archive &mdash; <span class="mono">tech.anujsadani.in/the-meter-is-not-the-money</span>
      &mdash; holding a saved copy of every source it cites, so that any claim
      can be checked years from now even if the original page has moved or
      vanished. Those saved copies remain the property of the publishers who
      wrote them and are kept only as evidence.</p>
    <p>Prices in this industry change without announcement or changelog. Every
      price quoted here carries the date it was read, and the ledger records
      the access date and the cryptographic hash of the page it was read from.
      A price in these pages is evidence about a day, not a standing fact.</p>
    <p>Four of the figures in Section 2 reach this book at one remove: the
      original reporting on the purchase price sits behind paywalls that could
      not be captured, so the book cites the outlets that relayed it and says
      so at each one. No primary document on the price was read.</p>

    <h3>A note on how this book was written</h3>

    <p>An AI system drafted this book. Not "assisted with" &mdash; drafted it,
      from the research through to the prose you are reading. I set the
      question and its boundaries, made the judgment calls, argued with the
      drafts, and put my name on the result. The errors are mine; the model
      does not get to share the blame.</p>

    <p>The objection worth taking seriously is not that a machine wrote it. It
      is that nobody checked it. So this one was built the other way round:
      sources captured to disk and hashed before a word was drafted, every
      factual sentence bound to an exact quotation inside one of those files,
      and a verification gate that refused to let a sentence exist unless its
      quotation could be located in a stored copy of the source.</p>

    <p>Seventy-seven claims went into the ledger. Statements that could not be
      located in a source did not become sentences. Two quotations reached a
      late draft without a claim behind them and were bound before publication
      rather than quietly cut. One passage rested on a regulatory ruling from
      2003 that a later rulemaking had superseded; Section 6 says so in the
      text, because a book making this argument does not get to correct itself
      quietly.</p>

    <p>The superscripts are not decoration &mdash; they are the audit trail,
      and you can walk it yourself in an afternoon. Every one resolves to a row
      in the claim ledger naming the source, the exact quoted passage, and the
      date it was read.</p>
    <p>One more thing you should know before you start. The strongest argument
      against this book appears in Section 5, in its own words, with no
      rebuttal appended to soften it. I could not refute it with the evidence
      I gathered, and would rather say so here than hope you did not notice on
      the way past.</p>

    <p class="signoff">&mdash; A.S.</p>

    <p class="imprint">First edition &middot; August 2026</p>
  </div>

</section>
<!-- /BOOK-FORGE:FRONT-MATTER -->

